{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WMsdCt3UUvagdHEX8L11Jw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ACL ARR reviewer guidelines scoring rubric 2025 2026\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"Authors Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors\"},{\"title\":\"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing\"},{\"title\":\"aclrollingreview/cfp.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/cfp.md\"},{\"title\":\"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp\"},{\"title\":\"Future Improvements to ARR – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/future\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about ACL ARR reviewer guidelines and scoring rubric for 2025-2026:\n\n## Reviewer Scoring System\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n### Soundness Score\nSoundness scores must be justified by the text of the review. If you give a low Soundness score without finding any major faults, this means that your review is not a faithful explanation of your recommendation, and you need to revise it.\n\n### Excitement Score\nSometimes you may find the work sound, but not something that is interesting for you personally. Such preferences are more subjective and should be reflected in a separate excitement score, which is orthogonal to the assessment of the soundness of the paper.\n\n## Overall Recommendation Scores\n\nThe overall recommendation scale includes the following options:\n- 5 = Consider for Award: I think this paper could be considered for an outstanding paper award at an *ACL conference (up to top 2.5% papers).\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that can be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that cannot be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n- 1 = Do not resubmit: This paper has to be fully redone, or it is not relevant to the *ACL community (e.g. it is in no way related to computational processing of language).\n\nFor complete details, you can visit the official [ARR Reviewer Guidelines](http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines) and [Review Form](http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform) pages.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1de34d84-f766-4404-96e4-471a6ac5107f","uuid":"ca22f53a-0e3a-4fc6-b875-c1cb4a679d01","timestamp":"2026-05-21T07:09:36.109Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ACL ARR reviewer guidelines scoring rubric 2025 2026","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Nwpk2u4GKGwrX4Nr8dsNMm","content":[{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"Authors Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors"},{"title":"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing"},{"title":"aclrollingreview/cfp.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview","url":"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/cfp.md"},{"title":"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp"},{"title":"Future Improvements to ARR – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/future"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found about ACL ARR reviewer guidelines and scoring rubric for 2025-2026:\n\n## Reviewer Scoring System\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n### Soundness Score\nSoundness scores must be justified by the text of the review. If you give a low Soundness score without finding any major faults, this means that your review is not a faithful explanation of your recommendation, and you need to revise it.\n\n### Excitement Score\nSometimes you may find the work sound, but not something that is interesting for you personally. Such preferences are more subjective and should be reflected in a separate excitement score, which is orthogonal to the assessment of the soundness of the paper.\n\n## Overall Recommendation Scores\n\nThe overall recommendation scale includes the following options:\n- 5 = Consider for Award: I think this paper could be considered for an outstanding paper award at an *ACL conference (up to top 2.5% papers).\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that can be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that cannot be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n- 1 = Do not resubmit: This paper has to be fully redone, or it is not relevant to the *ACL community (e.g. it is in no way related to computational processing of language).\n\nFor complete details, you can visit the official [ARR Reviewer Guidelines](http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines) and [Review Form](http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform) pages."],"durationSeconds":7.860986726999996}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GFJH55uYFKHnQ8WJgpACx2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, I now have the ACL ARR scoring rubric. The key scores are:\n- Soundness (1-5)\n- Excitement (1-5) \n- Overall recommendation: 5 (Award), 4 (Accept), 3.5 (Leaning accept), 3 (Borderline), 2.5 (Leaning reject), 2 (Resubmit next cycle), 1.5 (Resubmit after next), 1 (Do not resubmit)\n\nWait, looking more carefully at the search result, I see scores 5, 2, 1.5, 1 — but there should be more in between. \n\nI have enough to work with for now. Let me set up the two reviewers to work in parallel — one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work. I've got all the context I need from the paper to get them started.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1282,"cache_read_input_tokens":59356,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1282},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1de34d84-f766-4404-96e4-471a6ac5107f","uuid":"7710d381-8dc0-40d6-a9ea-3a1da5a715bb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GFJH55uYFKHnQ8WJgpACx2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ACL ARR scoring rubric obtained. Now dispatching R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work) in parallel."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1282,"cache_read_input_tokens":59356,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1282},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1de34d84-f766-4404-96e4-471a6ac5107f","uuid":"9700a207-36e2-4a28-b505-65f09138a96e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GFJH55uYFKHnQ8WJgpACx2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AwPKLvJtxVbyFjC2QqdFcN","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for an ACL ARR review. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. \n\n## Scoring rubric (ACL ARR)\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 (5=flawless methodology, 1=fundamentally flawed)\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 (5=award-worthy, 1=uninteresting)\n- **Overall**: 5 (Award), 4 (Accept), 3.5 (Leaning accept), 3 (Borderline), 2.5 (Leaning reject), 2 (Resubmit next cycle), 1.5 (Resubmit after next cycle), 1 (Do not resubmit)\n\n## Paper summary\n\n**Title**: \"Psychometric DIF Analysis Reveals 31% Temporal Item Instability in MMLU: A Large-Scale Characterization of Benchmark Measurement Properties\"\n\nThe paper applies Mantel–Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items). Key findings:\n- 31.3% of items show large DIF (ETS Category C) between 2023 and 2024-H1 cohorts\n- Web-overlap contamination labels show enrichment≈1.0 (no correlation with DIF flags)\n- Semi-synthetic validation with cross-subject matching achieves ΔFPR=0.000\n- Despite 31% instability, rankings are stable (ρ≈0.997)\n- GSM8K replication: ΔTPR=+0.818, GSM1K r=−0.478 (N=33)\n\nThe paper runs 13 experiments total. Key results by experiment:\n\n**Exp 1 (Temporal DIF on MMLU)**: C%=31.3%, Li-clean FPR=0.318, enrichment=0.96, random-split sanity C%=0.18%, ability-matched C%=2.9%, within-family Llama-2 vs Llama-3 C%=61.3%, domain ρ=0.307 (p=0.02). IRT person-fit: 98% of models show |Lz|>2.\n\n**Exp 2 (Architecture Stratification)**: 5,226 decoder-only + 1 non-decoder. Jaccard=1.0 between full and decoder-only. RMSEA=0.087, CFI=0.73. A vacuous check since only 1 non-decoder model exists.\n\n**Exp 3 (Semi-Synthetic Validation)**: ΔTPR dose-response: p=0.3→+0.222, p=0.5→+0.508, p=1.0→+0.674. Baseline FPR=0.314. Cross-subject matching ΔFPR=0.000 (vs standard matching +0.318 inflation). Ability-split: TPR@p=1.0=0.80, FPR=0.493.\n\n**Exp 4 (GSM8K Cross-Benchmark)**: Standard C%=18.9%, external C%=47.4%, sanity C%=0.3%. Semi-synthetic ΔTPR=0.818. GSM1K: r=−0.478 (p=0.005), ρ=−0.403 (p=0.020), BCa CI [−0.714, −0.155], partial r=−0.385. N=33 models only.\n\n**Exp 5 (RL Robustness)**: N=13 GRPO/PPO models. AUROC: DIF 0.58 [0.41,0.73], ConStat 0.54, Min-K% 0.51. DeLong p=0.38. TOST p=0.22. Power at p=0.4: 0.12. Exploratory, underpowered.\n\n**Exp 6 (ConStat Comparison)**: 220 reformulated items. ConStat FPR=0.067 vs DIF FPR=0.318. Overlap=12.7%. ConStat detection rate: non-RL 0.41, RL 0.08.\n\n**Exp 7 (DVD)**: 12 models × 200 items × 50 gens. Bimodal detection=0.14, variance-based=0.22. DVD-DIF overlap=0.09. FPR=0.04. 65 GPU hours.\n\n**Exp 8 (Ensemble)**: Majority vote precision=0.38, recall=0.09. Weighted: precision=0.44, recall=0.15. ΔF1(optimized−uniform)=0.02. Weight stability std/mean>0.6. DIF weight=0.28, ConStat=0.51, DVD=0.21. Negative result.\n\n**Exp 9 (Bifactor IRT)**: Failed. Non-convergence, 47 Heywood cases. RMSEA=0.089, CFI=0.71, TLI=0.68, SRMR=0.112. 0 anchor items. Pipeline dead.\n\n**Exp 10 (Item Generation)**: 500 items. Difficulty ρ(prompt)=0.18, ρ(student-sim)=0.52 (target 0.75). Construct alignment=0.64 (target >0.80). Holdout ρ=0.41 (target >0.70). All below benchmarks.\n\n**Exp 11 (Cumulative Bayesian DIF)**: Simulation only. Power: 1 admin=0.38, 2=0.49, 3=0.58, 4=0.64 (target 0.70). No real-data validation.\n\n**Exp 12 (Graceful Degradation)**: ρ@20% replacement=0.961, α drops 0.94→0.88. 3 subjects lose >30% items. Simple removal achieves ρ≈0.997 — better than replacement because replacement items add noise.\n\n**Exp 13 (Item-Focused Trees)**: MH flags 3,918 (31.3%), IFT flags 4,102 (32.8%). Jaccard=0.48. First split=release_date. Interaction with model size p<0.01. 52% method disagreement.\n\n## Experiment design reference\n\nThe full experiment plan includes these 13 experiments covering: real_data_dif_mmlu (plan_001), rl_robustness_validation (plan_002), constat_perturbation_eval (plan_003a), dvd_generation_variance_eval (plan_003b), ensemble_fusion (plan_003c), cross_benchmark_gsm8k (plan_004), bifactor_irt_equating_pilot (plan_005), item_generation_calibration (plan_006), cumulative_bayesian_dif (plan_007), graceful_degradation_analysis (plan_008), item_focused_trees (plan_009), architecture_stratified_dif (plan_010), semi_synthetic_contamination_validation (plan_011).\n\nAll experiments are covered in the paper. The paper is comprehensive in experiment coverage.\n\n## Your task\n\nProvide a thorough R1 review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness** of each experiment\n2. **Statistical rigor** (sample sizes, confidence intervals, multiple testing, effect sizes)\n3. **Internal validity** (confounds, alternative explanations)\n4. **Experimental design** quality and completeness\n5. **Reproducibility** concerns\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: Critical/Major/Minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness, Excitement, Overall (with brief justification)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Report in under 1500 words."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1282,"cache_read_input_tokens":59356,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1282},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1de34d84-f766-4404-96e4-471a6ac5107f","uuid":"d360cd63-c328-48ae-b924-cc8527791e6a"}
